          "0": {
            "id": "14013472",
            "headline": "Can the Skyways IPO go sky-high?",
            "heading": "Can the Skyways IPO go sky-high?",
            "is_premium": "1",
            "intro": "Strong revenue growth and strategic expansion offer upside, but thin margins and high working-capital needs remain key risks",
            "body": "Highlights\r\n\r\n\tRs 583 crore IPO; combination of fresh issue and OFS\r\n\tThe issue implies a market capitalisation of Rs 2,006 crore\r\n\tA multi-modal logistics company with air cargo as the core business\r\n\tRevenues and profits have scaled rapidly, but operates on thin margins\r\n\tEntry into adjacent segments of the logistics value-chain offers additional growth optionality\r\n\tStructural tailwinds, reasonable valuation could garner investor interest\r\n\r\nIndia\u2019s rising consumption, manufacturing activity, e-commerce, and exports are driving greater movement of goods, creating a structural growth opportunity for the logistics industry. Skyways Air Services (SASL), a four-decade-old freight-forwarding company, intends to leverage its IPO to capture this market opportunity. The Rs 583-crore IPO comprises a Rs 399-crore fresh issue and a Rs 184-crore OFS.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nA diversified freight forwarder, but air freight remains mainstay\r\n\r\nFounded in 1984, Skyways has evolved from an air-freight specialist into a multi-modal logistics platform, offering air and ocean freight forwarding, trucking, warehousing, express cargo, customs broking, and other supply-chain services. The asset-light model relies on relationships with airlines, ground-handling agents, and logistics partners rather than ownership of significant transportation infrastructure.\r\n\r\nThis diversification is strategically useful, with ocean freight contributing ~16 percent of revenue, reducing dependence on air cargo while allowing Skyways to offer customers an integrated supply chain platform. The express business is another emerging growth engine, with revenue increasing sharply at a 66 percent CAGR, from Rs 58 crore in FY24 to Rs 162 crore in FY26. However, air freight remains the core business, making the company exposed to freight rates, airline capacity, and global trade conditions.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nScale and relationships are the key differentiators\r\n\r\nSkyways claims the No. 1 position among Indian air-freight forwarders by AWB (Air Waybills) generation for the last four years.\r\n\r\nThe company has performance-based arrangements with leading global airlines, including Emirates, Saudi Cargo, Air India Cargo, and Lufthansa, while also being part of global freight-forwarding networks such as the World Cargo Alliance (WCA) and Global Freight Alliance (GFA).\r\n\r\nThese relationships provide access to international routes and cargo capacity while strengthening Skyways' ability to serve customers across geographies. More importantly, its accumulated experience and network can provide an advantage in a fragmented and operationally complex industry, particularly during periods of disruption when trade routes, airline capacity, or freight movements are affected.\r\n\r\nThe market opportunity is large, but volume growth alone may not be enough\r\n\r\nIndia's logistics industry is supported by several structural tailwinds, including manufacturing and export growth, rising e-commerce penetration, supply-chain formalisation, increasing 3PL/4PL adoption, cold-chain development, infrastructure investments, and greater adoption of multimodal transportation. These trends should support sustained growth in freight and logistics demand.\r\n\r\nHowever, freight forwarding is inherently a low-margin business, meaning that higher shipment volumes do not necessarily translate into proportionate earnings growth. For Skyways, the larger opportunity may therefore lie in increasing value per shipment and expanding its share of the logistics value chain, rather than simply pursuing higher freight volumes.\r\n\r\nMoving up the value chain is critical\r\n\r\nSkyways primarily operates in the air cargo segment, which accounts for only a relatively small portion of India's overall logistics activity. Moreover, it operates in a business where several important variables remain outside its control, including airline capacity, freight rates, fuel costs, global trade conditions, and geopolitical disruptions.\r\n\r\nThe company is therefore expanding into higher-value services such as warehousing, cold chain, express logistics, and supply-chain management.\r\n\r\nA potentially meaningful long-term opportunity is its planned entry into cargo infrastructure and terminal management, including cargo terminals, container freight stations (CFS), inland container depots (ICDs), logistics parks, and related infrastructure.\r\n\r\nImportantly, Skyways has entered into a consortium with Swissport International AG to bid for the development of a new cargo terminal at Kolkata airport under a Design, Build, Finance, Operate and Transfer (DBFOT) model.\r\n\r\nIf successful, such initiatives could potentially offer better economics, higher customer stickiness, and greater control over the logistics value chain. More importantly, they could gradually transform Skyways from primarily being a freight intermediary into a company that also owns/operates strategic logistics infrastructure. However, this remains an optionality rather than an established earnings driver.\r\n\r\nGrowth has been strong, but margins remain thin\r\n\r\nSkyways\u2019 revenue has increased from Rs 1,021 crore in FY24 to Rs 2,166 crore in FY26, implying a ~48 percent CAGR. Similarly, EBITDA has grown at a 62 percent CAGR over the same period.\r\n\r\nHowever, freight forwarding is a high-volume, low-margin business, and Skyways operates with an operating margin of only around 4 percent. Consequently, even modest movements in freight rates, operating costs, or competitive intensity can have a disproportionate impact on profitability.\r\n\r\nThe investment thesis therefore depends not only on revenue growth but also on the company\u2019s ability to increase the contribution of higher-value services and improve its margin profile.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWorking capital is the biggest financial determinant\r\n\r\nAs a freight forwarder, Skyways typically need to pay airlines, shipping lines first and collect from customers later. This creates substantial receivables and working-capital requirements and an important distinction where the net profit doesn\u2019t translate into equivalent free cash flow.\r\n\r\nConsolidated borrowings stood at ~Rs 625 crore in FY26, implying debt/equity of 1.9x. The company\u2019s working capital estimates stand at Rs 563 crore and Rs 685 crore for FY27 and FY28, respectively.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nWhile a substantial portion of the IPO proceeds will be deployed towards debt repayment and working capital requirement; the underlying business model will continue to require significant funding as the company grows.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nValuation and investment view\r\n\r\nAt the upper price band, Skyways is valued at ~32x FY26 P/E, which is at a discount to many of its peers, which is warranted in our view, given the thin margin and high leverage position.\r\n\r\nThat said, the small base of Skyways allows for much faster growth potential along with the optionality from the strategic entry into management and maintenance of cargo terminals. The IPO offers an attractive growth story driven by structural tailwinds and a relatively reasonable valuation, providing scope for listing gains.\r\n\r\nThe long-term thesis will, however, depend on whether Skyways can translate its strong revenue growth into higher margins and better cash conversion, which will remain the key determinant.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor\u00a0more\u00a0research\u00a0articles, visit our Moneycontrol\u00a0Research\u00a0page",
